Fabricator local patch for wry: WebView2 device-compliance SSO

Turns on the WebView2 environment option AllowSingleSignOnUsingOSPrimaryAccount so
the embedded preview presents the device's Microsoft Entra identity (Primary Refresh
Token / device certificate). Deployed apps behind a Conditional Access "require
compliant/managed device" policy then sign in inside the preview instead of failing
with "You can't get there from here". Upstream wry builds the environment options but
exposes no setter for this one, and it is an environment option, not a browser
argument, so it can't be passed through Tauri.

Where: src/webview2/mod.rs, InnerWebView::create_environment, right after
options.set_are_browser_extensions_enabled(...).
Docs: docs/VENDORED-WRY-PATCH.md

diff --git a/src/webview2/mod.rs b/src/webview2/mod.rs
--- a/src/webview2/mod.rs
+++ b/src/webview2/mod.rs
@@ -327,6 +327,16 @@ impl InnerWebView {
       options.set_additional_browser_arguments(additional_browser_args);
       options.set_are_browser_extensions_enabled(pl_attrs.browser_extensions_enabled);
 
+      // [rayfin-desktop local patch: webview2-sso] Share the OS primary
+      // (Entra-joined) account with this WebView2 environment. This surfaces the
+      // device-bound Primary Refresh Token / device certificate to Microsoft Entra
+      // ID, so deployed apps gated by a Conditional Access "require
+      // compliant/managed device" policy can sign in inside the embedded preview
+      // instead of failing with "You can't get there from here". Upstream wry
+      // leaves this `false` and exposes no setter, hence the vendored patch (see
+      // docs/VENDORED-WRY-PATCH.md and src-tauri/vendor/wry-patches).
+      options.set_allow_single_sign_on_using_os_primary_account(true);
+
       // Get user's system language
       let lcid = GetUserDefaultUILanguage();
       let mut lang = [0; MAX_LOCALE_NAME as usize];
